-- title:
--   Connectivity in the favored graph of a town (Definition 5.13)
-- statement:
--   **Definition 5.13 of Bux–Kassmann–Schulze**, the *favored graph* of a town, as a
--   connectivity relation.
--
--   A cone $V$ is **favored by majority** in a block $Q$ (Definition 5.11) when its fibre
--   $\{x \in Q : \Gamma(x) = V\}$ has maximal size among all double cones. Definition 5.13
--   puts a directed edge from a block $Q$ to a block $P$ when there is *some* cone $V$
--   favored by majority in $Q$ with $y \in V[x]$ for all $x \in Q$ and all $y \in P$ — this
--   is `QFS.FavoredEdge` — and calls the corresponding undirected graph the favored graph.
--
--   `FavoredConn Γ T` is the reflexive-transitive closure of the relation "$Q$ and $P$ both
--   lie in $T$, and there is a favored edge one way or the other". So
--   `FavoredConn Γ T Q P` says that $Q$ and $P$ are joined by an undirected edge path in the
--   favored graph **all of whose blocks lie in $T$** — the confinement Proposition 5.14
--   asserts. Being a reflexive closure, it holds trivially for $Q = P$, and the step relation
--   carries no $Q \ne P$ condition.
--
--   The existential over favored cones sits **inside** the edge relation, which is the point
--   of Remark 5.12: the majority cone of a block is in general not unique, so different edges
--   of the same path may be witnessed by different cones.
